crossover-wheel, macOS arm64 binaries.

Force feedback works on a Thrustmaster T150 under macOS, from an
unprivileged process. Nothing here needs root except probe_intr, which is a
measurement tool rather than something you need.

Start here:

  ./t150boot            take the wheel out of boot mode. Run it on every
                        plug-in; sleep and wake put the wheel back.
  ./t150ctl status      what the wheel is
  ./t150ctl range 270   three quarters of a turn lock to lock
  ./t150ctl autocenter 0    let go of the wheel completely
  ./t150d -v            the daemon a game talks to

macOS will refuse to run these until you clear the quarantine flag, because
they are unsigned:

  xattr -d com.apple.quarantine ./*

Sit at the machine. IOHIDDeviceSetReport is gated on being the console user
and fails over SSH.

The man pages are here too. Read one with:

  man ./t150ctl.1

The daemon stops every effect and closes the wheel's input the moment it
takes the wheel. The open outlives whoever sent it, so a wheel left running
an effect by an earlier tool would otherwise keep rendering it. If a wheel
is ever moving on its own, starting and stopping t150d is the rescue, and a
replug lets it re-find its centre.

WHAT DOES NOT WORK YET. No game has reached the wheel through this.
t150boot, t150ctl and t150d have all run against a real wheel and worked,
but the in-bottle proxy has never executed in a bottle, and the daemon has
never rendered an effect a client asked for. Only a constant force and one
periodic have ever been played on hardware: springs, dampers, envelopes and
per-effect gain are still derived from a Linux driver rather than measured.
CrossOver also registers none of the wheel's buttons, which is a separate
problem in macOS HID, SDL or winebus rather than in the wheel.

docs/PROBES.md in the repository is the procedure, and what each outcome
means.
